{"title":"06 Clustering and importance","description":"Cluster feature profiles and rank predictors by classifier importance.","nextTour":"07 Visualization","steps":[{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":18,"title":"What is not a feature","description":"`EXCLUDE_NON_FEATURE_COLS` — ID/metadata columns and the count columns that would trivially co-vary. `get_numeric_feature_columns` (line 25) subtracts these from the numeric columns and also drops zero-variance features."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":31,"title":"Correlation → distance","description":"`compute_correlation_and_distance` — the Pearson matrix between FEATURES (rows are features, not participants; that's the paper's question) and the distance `D = 1 − R` clipped to [0, 2] (line 40)."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":45,"title":"Hierarchical view","description":"`cluster_features_hierarchical` — squareform (line 53) feeds `linkage` (complete method default), cut at k=4 via `fcluster` (line 57). One of the two algorithms used to check the paper's four-domain claim."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":67,"title":"K-Means view","description":"`cluster_features_kmeans` — K-Means (k=4, `random_state=42`, `n_init=10`) over each feature's correlation profile; returns the assignment plus the silhouette score as the quality number."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":86,"title":"Choosing k","description":"`evaluate_cluster_elbow_and_silhouette` — inertia and silhouette for k = 2…6 (line 95 loop), written to `cluster_validation_metrics.csv`; the evidence behind k = 4."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/clustering.py","line":106,"title":"Naming the domains","description":"`assign_cluster_domain_names` — a keyword heuristic over each cluster's member names (lines 114–121) picks one of four domain labels. Known limitation: it collapses most clusters into one name — read member lists, not labels."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/importance.py","line":23,"title":"Building X and y","description":"`prepare_classification_data` drops unusable labels ('Other', 'unknown'), and median-imputes missing feature values (lines 42–43). Documented trade-off: imputation happens once, before CV — not per fold."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/importance.py","line":48,"title":"The CV loop","description":"`evaluate_classifier_cv` — stratified 10-fold (line 56). Per fold: fit the scaler on TRAIN ONLY (lines 66–68) so scale statistics never leak from test folds; then fit/predict and score accuracy, Cohen's kappa (line 77), and balanced accuracy (line 84)."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/importance.py","line":96,"title":"Gini importance ranking","description":"`compute_feature_importance_rf` — a Random Forest on the (scaled) full data; `feature_importances_` scaled to a 0–100 score (line 112), sorted with a Rank column. Top 20 = the paper's headline list."},{"file":"src/delware_speech/importance.py","line":124,"title":"The model benchmark","description":"`benchmark_all_models` — Random Forest vs SVM (RBF) vs Gradient Boosting (lines 130–134), each through the same leak-free CV; writes `model_benchmark_comparison.csv`."}]}